EBS store the data in encrypted format.

Consider you have a notebook

a) Normal disk:

EC2 → EBS
       ↓
    Data
       ↓
  readable

b) Encrypted EBS:

EC2 → EBS
       ↓
  🔐 Encrypted Data

Here data is not readable even if unauthorized person try to access the data.

=> This will be flow of our lab

Create encrypted EBS
        ↓
Attach to EC2
        ↓
Check encryption
        ↓
Format + mount
        ↓
Create test data
        ↓
Create snapshot
        ↓
Check snapshot encryption
        ↓
Create volume from snapshot
        ↓
Verify encryption

Step 1: Create encrypted EBS

AWS Console mein:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Click:

Create volume

Choose:

Volume type: gp3
Size: 10 GiB

Then encryption section mein:

Encrypt this volume: ✅

For this lab, use the AWS-managed/default EBS encryption key if the console offers it.

You may see something like:

KMS key
aws/ebs

=> What is KMS?

Very simple:

KMS = AWS service that manages encryption keys.

Think:

KMS
 ↓
Encryption key 🔑
 ↓
EBS
 ↓
Encrypted data 🔐

You don't need to learn KMS deeply right now.

Step 2: Same Availability Zone

Make sure the new EBS is in the same AZ as your EC2.

For example:

EC2
ap-south-1a
   │
   ↓
EBS
ap-south-1a

Then click Create volume.

Step 3: Check encryption

After creating the volume, select it.

You should see:

Volume type: gp3
Size: 10 GiB
Encrypted: Yes

This is your first verification.

Step 4: Attach it

Select the volume:

Actions → Attach volume

Select your EC2.

Then SSH into Ubuntu.

Run:

lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS

You'll find your new 10G disk.

For example:

nvme6n1    10G

⚠️ Don't assume nvme6n1. Your device name may be different.

Step 5: Find the exact EBS volume

Run:

ls -l /dev/disk/by-id/ | grep "vol"

Find the new volume ID and see which NVMe device it points to.

For example:

vol0abc123...
       ↓
/dev/nvme6n1

Step 6: Create filesystem

Because this is a brand-new EBS volume, we can format it.

Suppose the new device is:

/dev/nvme6n1

Then:

sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/nvme6n1

⚠️ Very important: Don't run this against:

/dev/nvme0n1

your root disk.

And don't run it against your existing:

/data-ebs
/restore
/data-lvm

Step 7: Mount

Create mount point:

sudo mkdir /data-encrypted

Mount:

sudo mount /dev/nvme6n1 /data-encrypted

Check:

df -h /data-encrypted

Step 8: Create test data
echo "This is encrypted EBS data" | sudo tee /data-encrypted/test.txt

Check:

cat /data-encrypted/test.txt

You should get:

This is encrypted EBS data

Step 9: Create snapshot

Now go to:

AWS Console → EC2 → Volumes

Select your encrypted EBS volume.

Choose:

Actions → Create snapshot

Give it a name such as:

lab6-encrypted-ebs-snapshot

Create it.

Step 10: Check snapshot encryption

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Snapshots

Find your snapshot.

You should see:

Encrypted: Yes

So the flow becomes:

Encrypted EBS
     ↓
Snapshot
     ↓
Encrypted Snapshot

Step 11: Restore from snapshot

Select the snapshot:

Actions → Create volume from snapshot

Create another EBS volume.

AWS will preserve the encryption.

So:

Encrypted EBS
      ↓
Encrypted Snapshot
      ↓
New Encrypted EBS

This is a very useful concept.

Note:- Is a snapshot of an encrypted EBS encrypted?

For an encrypted EBS snapshot, yes, the snapshot is encrypted.

Step 12: Check new restored volume in AWS

Go to:

EC2 → Elastic Block Store → Volumes

Find the new volume created from the snapshot.

Check:

Volume type → gp3
Size        → 10 GiB
Encrypted   → Yes
State       → Available

If it shows Encrypted = Yes, the encryption part is confirmed.

Step 13: Attach the restored volume

Select the new volume:

Actions → Attach volume

Select your EC2 instance.

Then SSH into Ubuntu.

Run:

lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS

Find the new 10 GiB disk.

For example:

nvme7n1   10G   ext4

⚠️ Your device may have a different name. Don't assume nvme7n1.

Step 14: Mount the restored volume

First create a mount point:

sudo mkdir /restore-encrypted

Then, assuming the new device is /dev/nvme7n1:

sudo mount /dev/nvme7n1 /restore-encrypted

Check:

df -h /restore-encrypted

You should see:

/dev/nvme7n1    10G    ...    ...    ...    /restore-encrypted

Step 15: Check the recovered data

This is the most important verification.

Run:

ls -l /restore-encrypted

You should see your previous test file:

test.txt

Then:

cat /restore-encrypted/test.txt

Expected:

This is encrypted EBS data

=> Now you've proved:

Encrypted EBS
      ↓
Encrypted Snapshot
      ↓
New EBS Volume
      ↓
Attach
      ↓
Mount
      ↓
Original data recovered

Important: For this restored volume, don't run mkfs.ext4. The filesystem and data already came from the snapshot.
